## Ascribe documentation

The Markdown pages under `handbook/pages/` are Ascribe documentation: pages with YAML frontmatter, `@` directives, and `{key}` phrases, checked against the content model in `handbook/ascribe.toml`. The pages under `handbook/pages/security/` aren't: that folder is another project's, with its own `ascribe.toml` and rules.

Programmatic check: after editing a page, run `ascribe check <file> --format concise` and fix every error. Before you finish, run `ascribe check --config handbook`. `ascribe explain <code>` says what a problem means and how to fix it.

Before writing a page about a subject, run `ascribe search "<words>" --config handbook` to find where the pages already say it, as readers read them, and link to what's there or extend it instead of saying it again.

### Page types

A page says what type it is with `type:` in its frontmatter, first, and its other fields are its type's. A file or folder whose name starts with `_` is a fragment, which pages `@include`, not a page, and names no type.

- `page`: frontmatter `title` (string, required), `description` (string), `owner` (string, required)

### Phrases

Write the phrase (`{company}`), not the text it stands for.

- `{company}`: Lantern Labs
- `{product}`: Lantern
- `{help}`: #eng-help
- `{docs}`: https://docs.lantern.example/

### Directives this project uses

- `@id: <id>` on the line under a heading: an id for links to it that doesn't change with its text
- `@include: <file>.md`, or `@include: <file>.md#<id>` for one section: another file's content, in place
- `@note {type=<type>}: <text>`, or `@note:` … `@end` around several blocks: a callout. Here: types `note`, `tip`, `important`, `warning`, `caution`, `policy`
- `@steps` on the line above an ordered list: the list is a procedure
- `.<Title>` on its own line, then `@details:` … `@end`: collapsible content; the title is required
- `@team-contact {channel=…}: <id>`: A card naming a team and the chat channel to reach it

### Links

Link to the source file, with a relative path such as `../guides/install.md#configure`, not to the site's address. `ascribe outline <page>` lists a page's headings with their ids, and `ascribe link <target> --from <page>` says whether a link works and what to write.

### More

To see what a reader of the site gets, not only a reader of the Markdown, run `ascribe resolve <page> --build <name> --emit site`, or call `ascribe_resolve` with `emit: "site"`: the page as the site output gives it to the site, with Ascribe's elements. How to read them: https://ascribed-dev.com/go/reading-site-output/

`ascribe model handbook` shows everything the content model allows. Every directive: https://ascribed-dev.com/go/directives/
